  "phenomenon": "On a March morning before the long rains, the Kenya Meteorological Department forecasts a 70% chance of rain in Kericho and the Rift Valley. A maize farmer near Eldoret must decide whether to plant today or wait or irrigate. At the same time, a pilot at JKIA checks the storm probability before planning a flight to Kisumu, and the Nairobi county disaster office decides whether to prepare drainage and warn residents of possible floods. The atmosphere is complex and constantly changing, so meteorologists cannot say for sure what will happen; they express how likely a weather event is using probability. It is puzzling because the forecast is never certain: a 70% chance of rain still means it might stay dry, and a 30% chance might still bring rain. Why do farmers, pilots and cities trust and act on numbers that do not guarantee the outcome, and how are such numbers worked out and combined?",
