  "storylineThread": "Lesson 1: Experimental probability I – Learners toss coins and bottle tops and predict how often 'rain' (heads) occurs; they record frequencies and meet relative frequency as a way to estimate likelihood, linking to forecasts built from past weather records.\nLesson 2: Experimental probability II – Learners increase the number of trials, pool class data and see relative frequency stabilise; they use past rainfall records from Kericho to estimate the probability of rain.\nLesson 3: Probability space I – Learners list all possible outcomes (sample space) for coins, dice and simple weather states (rain, no rain), and define events as subsets.\nLesson 4: Probability space II – Learners calculate theoretical probability from the probability space, compare it with experimental results and explain the differences, revising the forecast model.\nLesson 5: Mutually exclusive and independent events – Learners classify weather and everyday events (rain and sunshine at the same time; rain on Monday and Tuesday) as mutually exclusive, independent or neither.\nLesson 6: Laws of probability I – Learners apply the addition law P(A or B) for mutually exclusive events to find the chance of rain or storm for a farmer's decision.\nLesson 7: Laws of probability II – Learners apply the multiplication law P(A and B) for independent events to find the chance of rain on two consecutive days.\nLesson 8: Probability tree diagrams – Learners draw tree diagrams for combined events like the weather over two days and compute the probabilities of the branches and outcomes.\nLesson 9: Applying probability to real decisions – Learners use their complete model to advise the farmer, pilot and county disaster team, finalise the DQB and answer: How is probability applied in real life situations?"
