      "learned": "We learned that probability is a number from 0 to 1 that measures how likely an event is. It can be estimated from relative frequency or found by theoretical probability. We learned to list sample spaces, to use the addition law for mutually exclusive events, the multiplication law for independent events, the complement rule and tree diagrams. We also learned to compare probabilities with a decision threshold and with the cost of being wrong.",
      "explained": "A forecast of 70% chance of rain does not guarantee rain. It means that in a long run of similar days about 7 out of every 10 have rain, worked out from past records and models. Because these numbers come from many past trials, Wanjiru can prepare to plant and keep irrigation as backup, Captain Otieno can plan for a possible 10% storm and check again before take-off, and the county office can prepare drainage and warn residents. A decision based on probability may be unlucky on one day, but it is the best choice over many days. This is how probability is applied in real life situations."
